<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hulagu Khan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1217–<a href="/years/1265">1265</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Hulagu · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borjigin">Borjigin</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hulagu Khan was a Mongol prince, a grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a>, who led the Mongol campaigns into south-western Asia in the 1250s. In 1258 his forces sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> and executed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>, ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a>. He founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a>, which ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and neighbouring lands.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hulagu-khan.jpg" alt="Hulagu Khan" width="281" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rashid-al-Din Hamadani · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hulagu_Khan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Hulagu Khan</b>'s forces took the city in February 1258, executed Caliph al-Musta'sim, and sacked its libraries, including the House of Wisdom.</blockquote><a href="/empire/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Smbat's embassy to Karakorum brought back in 1250 a guarantee of Cilicia's integrity; Het'um I visited Möngke Khan in 1253 and fought under <b>Hulagu</b> at Aleppo and Damascus in 1259–1260, where Arab historians charge his troops with a massacre and arson in the main mosque.</blockquote><a href="/empire/armenian-kingdom-of-cilicia">Armenian Kingdom of Cilicia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Ilkhanate grew out of the westward expansion of the Mongol Empire under <b>Hulagu Khan</b>, a grandson of Genghis Khan.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Hülegü</b>'s campaign in the Middle East destroyed Baghdad in 1258.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Mongol campaigns against the Khwarazmian Empire in 1219–1221 destroyed cities such as Urgench, Merv and Nishapur, and in 1258 <b>Hulagu Khan</b>’s army sacked Baghdad and put the last Abbasid caliph, al-Musta’sim, to death.</blockquote><a href="/government/nomadic-confederation">Nomadic confederation · Weaknesses</a></li><li><blockquote>After a short siege, the Abbasid Caliph al-Musta'sim surrendered Baghdad to <b>Hulagu Khan</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-10">10 February in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maragha">Maragheh</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.39,46.24&amp;year=1248" aria-label="Maragheh on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5706 818 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Hulagu Khan"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6156 1088h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6156 1088h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=37.39,46.24&amp;year=1248">What was here in 1248? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
